          "worked_solution": "Recall the following:\n\n- If an integer $n$ is even, then there exists an integer $p$ such that $n=2p.$\n\n- If an integer $n$ is odd, then there exists an integer $q$ such that $n=2q+1.$\n\nIn this case, we need to prove the following:\n\n$$\n{n}^{3}\\text{ is even}\\,⇒\\,n\\text{ is even}\n$$\n\nTo prove $P⇒Q,$ it suffices to prove the contrapositive $¬Q⇒¬P.$ So, let's write down the contrapositive of our statement.\n\nThe contrapositive form of the given statement is as follows:\n\n$$\nn\\text{ is odd}\\,⇒\\,{n}^{3}\\text{ is odd}\n$$\n\nWe'll prove this statement instead of the original one.\n\nWe start by using the fact that $n$ is odd.\n\nIf $n$ is odd, then $n=2a+1$ for some integer $a.$\n\nThe idea is to substitute $n=2a+1$ into the expression ${n}^{3}$ and show it is odd by writing it as a multiple of $2,$ plus $1.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{3} & =(2a+1)^{3} \\\\  & =8{a}^{3}+12{a}^{2}+6a+1 \\\\  & =(8{a}^{3}+12{a}^{2}+6a)+1 \\\\  & =2{{(4{a}^{3}+6{a}^{2}+3a)}_{⏟}}_{b}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if we define another integer $b=4{a}^{3}+6{a}^{2}+3a$ (highlighted above), we have that ${n}^{3}=2b+1,$ which shows that ${n}^{3}$ is indeed one more than a multiple of $2.$\n\nLet $b=4{a}^{3}+6{a}^{2}+3a.$ Since $a$ is an integer, we have that $b$ is an integer.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{3} & =2{{(4{a}^{3}+6{a}^{2}+3a)}_{⏟}}_{b}+1 \\\\  & =2b+1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is one larger than a multiple of $2.$\n\nFinally, we write our conclusion.\n\nTherefore, ${n}^{3}$ is odd.",
